WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
RCEs are quite strange because on one hand,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.320
they are possible in many different functionalities,

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.560
but in practice, they are quite rare and very valuable.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.840
So if you want to improve in finding RCEs,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.200
you're in a good place because in this video,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.920
I will show you in what functionalities I observed them

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.920
during my case study of 126 disclosed bug bounty reports.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.100
Enjoy.

00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:30.900
So this was definitely the most difficult case study

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.540
because I always try to somehow aggregate

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.419
those labels together,

00:00:35.419 --> 00:00:40.099
but RCEs were completely all over the place.

00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:43.259
So in total, I have 13 functionalities

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:45.259
and we'll go through them one by one.

00:00:45.259 --> 00:00:47.860
And the first, the most common one

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.980
is some kind of import or sharing functionality.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.540
And it's definitely something

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.700
that you should pay attention to

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.619
as one of the most critical functionalities.

00:00:57.619 --> 00:01:01.860
For example, we had a lot of those bugs in GitLab

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.440
where for example, you can migrate a repository

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.099
from one GitLab instance to another.

00:01:08.099 --> 00:01:10.800
And it's a very dangerous functionality

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.360
because you have to copy some files

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.980
from one place to another.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.379
And every single interaction with a file

00:01:18.379 --> 00:01:21.860
is a place where RCEs are possible.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.339
Here, for example, to check a size of the archive,

00:01:26.339 --> 00:01:30.000
the system level command would be executed.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.279
And it is quite a complex report.

00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:35.980
We will not go into the details of it.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.559
But the bottom line is,

00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:39.860
it was just quite a complex functionality.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.720
There was a lot of source code review involved here.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.900
But in the end, the payload is not so complex.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.820
It just, the simplest escaping of the argument

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.620
and it could inject your own comments.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.739
So similar thing here in GitHub this time.

00:01:57.739 --> 00:01:59.540
That one was in GitLab.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.500
This one is in GitHub.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.940
Here as well, you have the processing of the file.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.020
This time it's GitHub pages, creating GitHub pages.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.479
And it would process YAML files

00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:15.460
with apparently some insecure configuration,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.300
which would allow you to create an arbitrary file.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.179
And if you can create arbitrary files,

00:02:22.179 --> 00:02:27.179
you can often get the RCE quite easily.

00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:31.399
And in the case here, this report was 25K.

00:02:31.399 --> 00:02:36.300
The previous one was $33,000.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.539
We'll now go to the second most common category,

00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:41.660
which unfortunately is called other

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:46.660
because these, I just couldn't fit to anything else.

00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:51.619
For example, you have this bug,

00:02:51.619 --> 00:02:56.619
which I would never think that this is something possible.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.580
We have exposed the Git repository

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.660
so that the hunter could see the source code.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.559
Nice that he also included the recon steps that he took.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.179
And turned out that the source code looked like this.

00:03:10.179 --> 00:03:13.860
You had some hard-coded secrets in the source code.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.899
And if those secrets were correct,

00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:19.940
the application would just execute system level comments

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.380
without any escaping.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.660
And normally without those secrets, you couldn't do it.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.699
But yeah, just leaking the secret was enough

00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:32.059
to execute arbitrary comments here.

00:03:32.059 --> 00:03:34.059
And it's interesting because I saw this report

00:03:34.059 --> 00:03:37.539
and I thought it's like 2015 or 2016.

00:03:37.539 --> 00:03:41.919
It's 2023 and it's $10,000 of bounty.

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:45.279
And yeah, most of these cases in the other functionality,

00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:50.240
in the other category is something quite specific.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.839
Then we have file uploads.

00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:56.139
And when we take a look here at those average bounties,

00:03:56.139 --> 00:03:58.839
we can see that the average bounty for file uploads

00:03:58.839 --> 00:04:02.820
is a bit lower than those above it and below it.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.080
But the difference is not huge.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.279
But when we take a look at them here,

00:04:08.279 --> 00:04:12.080
we can see bounties are not so great in this category.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.800
And the reason is many of them are just

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.040
the simplest web shell uploads

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.279
where the application would do no verification.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:23.079
And it often happens in smaller applications,

00:04:23.079 --> 00:04:25.760
in applications maybe where the company

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.100
doesn't have a proper security team.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.239
But the average bounty is bumped up

00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:35.239
by this 50,000 bounty from Apple.

00:04:35.239 --> 00:04:37.760
But if we take this one away,

00:04:37.839 --> 00:04:39.799
let's do it like this.

00:04:39.799 --> 00:04:41.000
If we take this one away,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.720
the average bounty goes down to $2,700,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.359
which is way less than anything else here.

00:04:49.359 --> 00:04:51.279
And before you ask me,

00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:53.760
because every single video like this has a comment,

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.260
can I access this database with those reports?

