But coming back to our reports,

00:06:16.679 --> 00:06:21.679
you're probably curious what is this $50,000 report to Apple?

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:24.299
And I also covered it on my channel.

00:06:24.299 --> 00:06:27.239
I will link all the videos that I mentioned

00:06:27.239 --> 00:06:29.019
in the description down below.

00:06:29.859 --> 00:06:34.019
And it was functionality or actually the framework.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.359
Lucy, I've never heard about it,

00:06:37.359 --> 00:06:40.899
but they were able to convince the Lucy

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.739
with some parameters and some templates

00:06:43.739 --> 00:06:45.700
to create an arbitrary file.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:49.380
And from there, the shell was possible.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.220
So it's also a shell upload,

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:56.220
but it's definitely not an easy one or a simple one.

00:06:56.299 --> 00:06:58.160
Next, we have authentication.

00:06:58.239 --> 00:07:00.640
And authentication, I know there are a lot of bugs there,

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.720
but I just wouldn't expect RCEs to be precise.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.640
And there were some, I would say,

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.079
random ones like this one,

00:07:09.079 --> 00:07:12.359
where the application would just take the value

00:07:12.359 --> 00:07:16.040
from the cookie and evaluate it.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.200
So we have the payload here.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.940
I'm not sure if this screenshot is visible,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.399
but there is base64 encoded command here,

00:07:24.399 --> 00:07:26.839
which is just executed by the server.

00:07:26.839 --> 00:07:29.679
And unfortunately, I cannot give you a recipe

00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:31.279
on how to find bugs like this

00:07:31.279 --> 00:07:35.040
because it just looks completely random to me.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.160
And I definitely would not find this bug

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.359
without the source code.

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:41.839
But one pattern in authentication that I saw,

00:07:41.839 --> 00:07:45.880
and I want you to know about it because it can be useful,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.880
is that sometimes the session cookie is a serialized object.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.279
And it's dangerous because if you could

00:07:54.279 --> 00:07:57.519
get your arbitrary objects to be deserialized,

00:07:57.519 --> 00:07:59.279
you could get an RCE.

00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:02.119
So to prevent this, all modern frameworks

00:08:02.119 --> 00:08:05.000
sign those cookies cryptographically

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.480
so that you cannot create your own cookie

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.859
and get it to deserialize.

00:08:08.859 --> 00:08:10.880
You need the signature to be valid.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:14.920
So in practice, you need the server to generate the cookie.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:15.880
But the thing is,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:21.320
if you can disclose the secret used to sign the cookie,

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.040
you can get an RCE.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.000
For example, here we have verbose errors in Rails,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.179
in the Rack framework.

00:08:29.179 --> 00:08:32.520
And the verbose error disclosed the cookie signing secret.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.479
So in this case, if you find something like this,

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:36.960
not report it straight away.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.919
Do not report that the application disclosed some secrets,

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.799
even if they look good.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.960
If you disclose the cookie signing secret,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.320
you should create your own malicious cookie

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.719
with a malicious object and sign it using that secret.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.119
And this will get you an RCE, like in this case.

00:08:56.119 --> 00:08:59.640
And this was a $5,000 reward.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.440
Then we have something that I called

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.719
high privilege functionality.

00:09:03.719 --> 00:09:06.599
And the difference here is that the functionality

00:09:06.599 --> 00:09:09.119
is supposed to execute code,

00:09:09.119 --> 00:09:11.799
just the attacker shouldn't be able to.

00:09:11.799 --> 00:09:16.799
For example, the first report that I have here,

00:09:17.919 --> 00:09:21.000
the functionality is like cloud-based stuff.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.559
So by default, you can execute code on your instance,

00:09:25.719 --> 00:09:27.440
but there was a CSRF.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.000
And CSRF doesn't sound like a $30,000 vulnerability,

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.159
but if the admin has access

00:09:35.159 --> 00:09:37.880
or the user has access to execute code,

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.239
and this functionality has a CSRF,

00:09:40.239 --> 00:09:45.239
then all of a sudden your CSRF is worth $30,000 like here.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.880
And the very interesting thing that we had here

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.400
was the origin validation,

00:09:50.799 --> 00:09:52.960
because there was some fuzzing involved.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.280
And the key here to bypass the origin check

00:09:58.440 --> 00:09:59.520
is this address.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.520
Turns out that this piece,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.119
which is the underscore subdomain,

00:10:06.119 --> 00:10:08.799
which looks a bit like the emoji,

00:10:08.799 --> 00:10:11.159
it was enough to, you know,

00:10:11.159 --> 00:10:14.799
that the website thought this is the origin

00:10:14.799 --> 00:10:19.280
and it looks valid because it ends with azurewebsites.net.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.760
But the browser thought that, you know,

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.719
this whole thing is the origin and this is attacker site.

00:10:26.719 --> 00:10:30.479
So a very nice CSRF, also nice write-up

00:10:30.479 --> 00:10:34.400
because he also disclosed what characters he tried

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.400
and what worked, what didn't.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.599
So really, really, really interesting case here

00:10:39.599 --> 00:10:41.719
and a really good bounty for this.

00:10:43.119 --> 00:10:45.799
Then we have a kind of similar case

00:10:45.799 --> 00:10:49.599
because we have cloud-based functionalities.

00:10:49.599 --> 00:10:50.640
And here as well,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.359
you can execute code on your own instance by default.

00:10:54.359 --> 00:10:56.520
But the thing here is the vulnerability

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:01.520
allowed either the escalation to instance of another user,

00:11:01.679 --> 00:11:06.080
or it allowed to like execute code on the host

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.520
of the, where you shouldn't be.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:13.520
Basically, you had a way to escape some kind of sandbox.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.280
And we'll not go into the details here

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.640
because we have a lot of bugs to cover,

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.239
which are closer to web-related bugs.

00:11:21.239 --> 00:11:24.119
For example, this first 30K attack was done

00:11:24.119 --> 00:11:27.719
via exposed kubelet, which is a Kubernetes sync.

00:11:27.719 --> 00:11:31.520
The second one was via exposed docker socket.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.320
So these are not really web bugs.

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:40.320
So we'll continue to the next category, which is templates.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.679
And most of these are server-side template injection bugs,

00:11:44.679 --> 00:11:48.159
which to be honest, when I learned about this vulnerability

00:11:48.159 --> 00:11:50.440
I thought it's much more common

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.960
and turns out it's actually not.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.840
This report is probably the most common example

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:58.479
that everyone uses when they want

00:11:58.479 --> 00:11:59.880
to show this vulnerability.

00:12:00.799 --> 00:12:04.080
It's in Uber found by Orange Tsai.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.159
Your name would be evaluated as a part of the template

00:12:08.159 --> 00:12:10.080
in the email you received.

00:12:10.919 --> 00:12:14.679
So you would inject something like this,

00:12:14.679 --> 00:12:17.479
which contains the Jinja2 templates.

00:12:17.479 --> 00:12:19.799
And when you would receive an email,

00:12:19.799 --> 00:12:23.840
it would be evaluated and it usually can lead

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.559
to a remote code execution.

00:12:25.559 --> 00:12:28.799
Interestingly, in this case, Orange was not able

00:12:28.799 --> 00:12:32.080
to exploit it as an RCE because of the length limit,

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.159
but Uber still probably evaluated it on their side

00:12:36.159 --> 00:12:40.520
and they paid $10,000 for this vulnerability.

00:12:41.359 --> 00:12:44.719
And the next category I have is also a way

00:12:44.719 --> 00:12:49.719
to exploit another vulnerability, which is SQL injection.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:53.799
I also talked about it in the SQL injection case study,

00:12:53.799 --> 00:12:55.679
which I recommend you to watch.

00:12:55.679 --> 00:12:57.520
I also, I'm going to link this

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:59.840
in the description down below.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:02.039
But the thing is, in some cases,

00:13:02.039 --> 00:13:04.880
in some configuration, with some configuration options

00:13:04.919 --> 00:13:08.039
and in some database engines,

00:13:08.039 --> 00:13:11.919
you are able to directly turn SQL injection

00:13:11.919 --> 00:13:16.359
into a remote code execution with a query like this one.

00:13:17.239 --> 00:13:21.159
And with this, the next functionality is installing packages.

00:13:21.159 --> 00:13:25.520
So it's a bit different because it often occurs earlier

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.200
in the development lifecycle.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.119
So these are not really bugs in the application itself,

00:13:31.119 --> 00:13:35.440
but somewhere before the application is even released.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:37.039
And here we have eight reports,

00:13:37.039 --> 00:13:41.239
but seven of those are dependency confusion vulnerability.

00:13:41.239 --> 00:13:43.799
This one, I also created a video about.

00:13:43.799 --> 00:13:47.840
It's a very interesting and novel attack scenario,

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.679
which Alex Bierson discovered in 2020

00:13:50.679 --> 00:13:53.479
or published in 2021.

00:13:53.479 --> 00:13:58.440
And it was basically a way to confuse a package manager

00:13:58.479 --> 00:14:01.440
to install your malicious package

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.359
instead of a company's internal package.

00:14:04.359 --> 00:14:07.479
So when they would use something like this, AuthPaypal,

00:14:07.479 --> 00:14:11.359
you could convince the package manager

00:14:11.359 --> 00:14:13.880
to install your AuthPaypal package,

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:16.440
which you can publish without any problems,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.799
because this one was private.

00:14:17.799 --> 00:14:20.760
It was in like private PayPal repository.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.479
So you published AuthPaypal to a public repository.

00:14:24.479 --> 00:14:27.280
And if your one would have a higher version,

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:31.359
so it would just use version 99999,

00:14:31.359 --> 00:14:32.960
it would install your package

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:37.440
and it would execute your code on the developer's machine,

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.760
maybe on the server as well.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.039
And bounties here were huge.

00:14:42.039 --> 00:14:45.000
He only mentioned three companies with bounties,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.159
and we have 40, 30, and 30K.

00:14:47.159 --> 00:14:49.960
So it's already $100,000.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:51.799
And he also mentioned a few more payouts,

00:14:51.799 --> 00:14:55.679
but didn't say exactly how much he got for them.

00:14:55.679 --> 00:14:56.919
So I couldn't put them here,

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:01.159
but definitely the attack worth it.

00:15:01.159 --> 00:15:06.080
And also here we have other dependency confusion reports,

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:08.719
also paid really, really well.

00:15:08.719 --> 00:15:11.760
And if you're wondering what's the one attack,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:15.159
which is not dependency confusion in this category,

00:15:15.159 --> 00:15:16.000
it's this one.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.320
Basically, Mapbox used some script

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.359
that pulled code from an S3 bucket,

00:15:22.359 --> 00:15:24.559
and then they stopped using the S3 bucket,

00:15:24.599 --> 00:15:29.599
but the script would still pull the code from this bucket.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.919
So Franz Rosen would claim this bucket,

00:15:32.919 --> 00:15:34.760
put his code there,

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:38.320
and the script would pull this code and execute it.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:43.320
So they paid $12,500 for this report.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.919
The next category I called zero day,

00:15:46.919 --> 00:15:50.559
and these are basically vulnerabilities in products

00:15:50.559 --> 00:15:53.159
and not in specific applications.

00:15:53.159 --> 00:15:57.359
For example, another report by Orange,

00:15:57.359 --> 00:15:59.440
this one, this time in Twitter.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.080
And these are vulnerabilities in the Pulse Secure VPN,

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:05.559
which Twitter used.

00:16:05.559 --> 00:16:08.679
And we can see six different CVEs here,

00:16:08.679 --> 00:16:12.280
really complex and really long explanation.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:16.679
But the bottom line is it ended in a remote code execution,

00:16:16.679 --> 00:16:18.559
and they got $20,000 for it.

00:16:18.559 --> 00:16:21.119
So bugs like this can be worth a lot,

00:16:21.119 --> 00:16:26.119
especially that if they had the vulnerability in the VPN,

00:16:26.559 --> 00:16:29.520
they could also report it to many other companies

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.799
that have bug bounty programs and that are using this VPN.

00:16:32.799 --> 00:16:37.799
So bugs like this can be really, really worth a lot.

00:16:38.599 --> 00:16:39.880
And another vulnerability

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:41.919
that you should pay a lot of attention

00:16:41.919 --> 00:16:44.080
is any kind of image processing,

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:48.400
or to be fair, processing of any file.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:51.359
Because we can see five of these

00:16:51.359 --> 00:16:53.080
are image magic vulnerabilities,

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:58.080
which you can see the dates, 2018, 18, 19, 16, 18.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:16:59.640
Quite an old vulnerability

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.799
that was very common in those days.

00:17:02.799 --> 00:17:06.959
Also in the SSRF case study, there were quite a lot of those

00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:12.880
but in any case, any functionality like this

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:14.800
should be your focus point.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.400
Here we have another vulnerability in GitLab.

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.719
I also covered it on my channel.

00:17:19.719 --> 00:17:24.160
And this is a zero day actually in the Exif tool,

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:29.160
which is used to strip metadata from a file.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:30.719
And it's a very interesting one

00:17:30.719 --> 00:17:35.719
because the code that was supposed to not make this possible

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:43.280
didn't predict that the new line just after a backslash

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:46.319
and would process it incorrectly,

00:17:46.319 --> 00:17:49.239
which could lead to you basically being able

00:17:49.239 --> 00:17:51.439
to inject your own code

00:17:51.439 --> 00:17:55.959
and execute your own code inside of a file.

00:17:55.959 --> 00:17:58.239
So also a really interesting one,

00:17:58.239 --> 00:18:02.040
also a lot of code review going on for this.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:05.400
I believe it's not possible to find a bug like this

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:07.160
with a black box approach,

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:08.560
but definitely worth it

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:13.160
because it's $20,000 from GitLab.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:16.319
Then there is another functionality,

00:18:16.319 --> 00:18:18.680
which I think it's quite obvious

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:21.319
and you probably won't be surprised

00:18:21.319 --> 00:18:25.880
that when the application executes command

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:28.479
as the functionality, it should be your focus point

00:18:28.479 --> 00:18:29.560
because it's quite natural

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.479
that when we see a command being executed,

00:18:32.479 --> 00:18:34.719
we immediately think about command injection.

00:18:34.719 --> 00:18:39.719
So I think it won't be the most interesting takeaway

00:18:40.319 --> 00:18:41.520
from this case study,

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.400
but take a look at this back.

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:47.640
It shows that not always you actually have

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:51.839
to escape the command to make a vulnerability work.

00:18:51.839 --> 00:18:56.119
Here we have another report by William Bowling

00:18:57.199 --> 00:18:58.959
and we have a functionality

00:18:58.959 --> 00:19:02.000
where it would run the Git command under the hood

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.040
to make some diff.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:06.880
And at the first sight, it doesn't look,

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.439
this part does not look sensitive at all.

00:19:10.479 --> 00:19:13.560
The file name was not vulnerable in this case.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.520
It was probably sanitized or something,

00:19:16.479 --> 00:19:21.479
but the commit one or commit two positional arguments,

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.040
they do not look like something

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.920
where you can inject your own comments.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:29.640
But the thing was that William created a branch

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.160
with a name like this,

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.239
a branch with a name dash dash output equals

00:19:34.239 --> 00:19:35.239
and a file name.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:39.199
And when it was put into the comment,

00:19:39.199 --> 00:19:44.199
then it was put here as commit one or commit two.

00:19:44.479 --> 00:19:47.920
And all of a sudden, instead of being a positional argument,

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:53.680
you were in the named argument called output

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:57.560
because still, even though he had a branch name like this,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.599
he couldn't just escape the argument

00:19:59.599 --> 00:20:01.199
and just inject arbitrary comments.

00:20:01.199 --> 00:20:03.560
It wasn't an easy case like this.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:08.560
He could just inject another argument into this comment

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:12.119
and this argument allowed him to create arbitrary file.

00:20:12.119 --> 00:20:15.719
And as you probably already know from this video,

00:20:15.719 --> 00:20:18.520
creating arbitrary file often allows you

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:22.520
to reach a remote code execution.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.520
And it was also case here.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:29.280
And now we'll step to the quite an interesting case

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.199
of a bug bounty program

00:20:31.199 --> 00:20:33.479
where all those three reports

00:20:33.479 --> 00:20:38.040
in the category I called SMTP DB configuration

00:20:38.079 --> 00:20:41.560
are 5K vulnerabilities in the same program called Ivan,

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.479
which I've never heard about.

00:20:43.479 --> 00:20:47.400
But what it does is it allows some configuration,

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:50.800
some database connectors or some SMTP connectors,

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:54.439
generally quite an advanced configuration.

00:20:54.439 --> 00:20:58.719
And those connectors were vulnerable in a very similar way.

00:20:58.719 --> 00:21:02.959
For example, here we have the SMTP server configuration

00:21:02.959 --> 00:21:06.119
and the password argument was vulnerable

00:21:06.119 --> 00:21:09.760
to a pretty standard common injection, I would say.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:14.760
And it was actually due via the new line injection.

00:21:15.239 --> 00:21:18.920
So you couldn't like inject the apostrophe or a quote.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.839
You could, the new line injection

00:21:22.839 --> 00:21:26.040
and the other two 5K reports for this program

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:28.439
were kind of similar.

00:21:29.359 --> 00:21:31.920
Next, we have a category called making requests.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:34.239
And if I really wanted,

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:36.359
this could be the most common category

00:21:36.359 --> 00:21:38.839
because in the SSRF case study,

00:21:38.839 --> 00:21:43.680
I observed, I think it was 28 SSRF reports

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:47.520
that resulted in leaking cloud metadata credentials.

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:49.040
And cloud metadata credentials

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:51.400
often allow you to execute code.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:53.640
So if I really wanted, I could copy reports

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:56.680
from that case study to this case study.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.319
And then making requests would be

00:21:59.319 --> 00:22:03.560
the most common functionality vulnerable to RCEs.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:07.760
But I decided that, you know, copying,

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:09.839
it won't add to the discussion a lot.

00:22:09.839 --> 00:22:12.119
So I only have three reports here

00:22:12.119 --> 00:22:15.000
where making requests or SSRFs,

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.560
because all these were SSRFs,

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.520
were directly turned to RCEs.

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:21.359
And I really like this report

00:22:21.359 --> 00:22:24.359
because in all the SSRF world lists

00:22:24.359 --> 00:22:28.160
or ways to escalate SSRFs,

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:31.160
you see the Gopher protocol being used.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:35.079
To be honest, I've never seen it work before this report.

00:22:36.079 --> 00:22:38.719
And I don't even know what it's supposed to do.

00:22:38.719 --> 00:22:43.160
But here it was used to achieve an RCE

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:46.040
because the Gopher protocol was used

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:51.040
to first detect the Redis instance on this port.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:57.040
And then this URL was used to run some Redis comments,

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:00.839
which allowed turning this SSRF into an RCE.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:03.280
So I really, really like this report.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:06.079
I really like to see the things

00:23:06.079 --> 00:23:07.800
which I always see in the world list,

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:09.199
and I don't really understand.

00:23:09.199 --> 00:23:10.599
I love to finally, you know,

00:23:10.599 --> 00:23:14.719
see them working in the real life.

00:23:14.719 --> 00:23:18.040
And finally, only one report in the functionality,

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:21.520
which I also think you should pay attention to

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:23.439
because it's a headless browser.

00:23:23.439 --> 00:23:27.280
When the server, you know, runs the browser

00:23:27.319 --> 00:23:30.079
to potentially render a PDF,

00:23:30.079 --> 00:23:32.880
to generate a report or do whatever.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:38.800
And here the Elastic or Kibana to be more specific,

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.760
they used a vulnerable version of Chromium.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:44.239
So it was vulnerable to,

00:23:44.239 --> 00:23:47.800
if you could run your arbitrary JavaScript code

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:50.880
in this browser, you could achieve an RCE.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:52.479
And the interesting thing is

00:23:52.479 --> 00:23:57.199
that this report is somewhat theoretical

00:23:58.119 --> 00:24:01.560
and every content about bug bounty

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:04.079
says to only report real threats.

00:24:05.439 --> 00:24:09.520
But here the hunter didn't have the way

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:13.239
to execute arbitrary JavaScript in the browser,

00:24:13.239 --> 00:24:15.079
but he decided to report it anyway.

00:24:15.079 --> 00:24:17.839
And he just wrote very honestly

00:24:17.839 --> 00:24:21.719
that there is no RCE at the moment,

00:24:21.719 --> 00:24:25.680
but you're just an XSS or HTML injection

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:29.000
or an open redirect away from the RCE

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:33.719
and Elastic decided to reward him with $10,000.

00:24:33.719 --> 00:24:38.199
So it just shows that in very specific situations,

00:24:38.199 --> 00:24:41.880
you can report vulnerabilities without a direct impact,

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:44.400
but you have to be very, very cautious with this.

00:24:45.359 --> 00:24:46.479
And that's it.

00:24:46.479 --> 00:24:47.839
That's all the functionalities

00:24:47.839 --> 00:24:50.199
that I observed during this case study.

00:24:50.199 --> 00:24:53.839
If you enjoyed this video with part of the case study,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.680
I encourage you to join BBRE Premium

